<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Douai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Douai is a town on the river Scarpe in the Nord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was one of the great cloth towns of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>; it passed to the French crown in 1312, returned to Flanders in 1369 and became French for good when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> took it in 1667. Its university, founded in 1559, was a centre of Catholic learning, and its English College produced the Douay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/douai.jpg" alt="Douai" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: JackyM59 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_Fontaine_de_la_Place_d%27Armes_de_Douai.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Douai lies on the river Scarpe, about 40 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lille">Lille</a> and 25 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arras">Arras</a>, and is a sub-prefecture of the Nord department. Its site probably corresponds to a 4th-century Roman fortress, Duacum. From the 10th century it was a fiefdom of the counts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. In English the town was historically spelled Douay or Doway.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town grew into a flourishing textile market centre in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Flanders passed to the Dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a> and in 1477 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>'s troops took Douai in 1667, and the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle of 1668 ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. During successive sieges from 1710 to 1712 the British Army almost completely destroyed it.</p><p>The University of Douai was founded under the patronage of the Spanish king while the town belonged to the Spanish Netherlands. From the 1560s until the French Revolution it was a centre for educating English Catholics escaping persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The English College, founded by William Allen, stood beside Irish and Scots Colleges and Benedictine, Franciscan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> houses. About 60 Jesuits went to England in the 1570s and around 500 by 1603, among them Edmund Campion and Robert Persons.</p><p>In 1609 the English College published a translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-testament">Old Testament</a> which, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> published at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Rheims</a> 27 years earlier, formed the Douay–Rheims <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, used almost exclusively by Anglophone Roman Catholics for more than 300 years. The Benedictine priory of St <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Gregory the Great</a> was founded at Douai by Saint John Roberts in 1605 and was expelled in 1793, settling at Downside Abbey in 1814. A second English Benedictine house took over its buildings in 1818 and returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1903 as Douai Abbey. The airfield at La Brayelle hosted the world's first aeronautical meeting in 1909.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Gothic belfry, begun in 1380 on the site of an earlier tower, is 80 metres high and holds a carillon of 62 bells. It was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2005 as part of the Belfries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Porte de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> gate dates from 1453, and the collegiate church of St Peter is 112 metres long. Chemical and metal engineering are the main industries, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renault">Renault</a> assembly plant has stood nearby since 1970.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Douai” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The county’s heartland was the Flemish-speaking north, but it also included French-speaking districts around Lille, <b>Douai</b> and Arras.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-flanders">County of Flanders · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
